\paragraph{Few-NERD $N$-scaling: MV at $N{=}16$.}
On Few-NERD (Qwen3-8B, 3-seed), increasing from $N{=}8$ to $N{=}16$ reduces degeneracy ($8.8$\%$\to$$4.0{\pm}1.4$\%) and attenuates MV degradation ($-2.74$\,pp$\to$$-1.65{\pm}0.61$\,pp), but MV remains negative.
Additional samples reduce the MV penalty but do not reverse it, consistent with the correlated-error mechanism: more samples provide better frequency estimates but cannot overcome systematic shared mistakes.
SJ $\rho$ is used for quality estimation at $N{=}16$ (FK conditional $\rho$ shows sign instability across seeds: mean${=}0.051{\pm}0.062$).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Effect of sample count on selection (SciERC NER, Qwen3-8B, $T{=}1.0$, gold-filtered, seed${=}42$; exp\_025).\protect\footnotemark\ ``Best $\Delta$'': highest selection gain across all 5 signals. Per-signal $\Delta$ in pp vs.~greedy.}
\label{tab:n_scaling_selection}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{
\begin{tabular}{ccccccccc}
\toprule
$N$ & Degen.\% & Oracle (pp) & LP & SJ & FK & EM & VC & Best $\Delta$ \\
\midrule
2 & 42.7\% & $+$4.4 & $-$0.89 & $-$2.72 & $-$2.72 & $-$2.72 & $-$2.66 & $-$0.89 \\
4 & 21.0\% & $+$10.2 & $+$0.03 & $-$0.31 & $-$0.40 & $-$0.74 & $-$2.16 & $+$0.03 \\
8 & 11.0\% & $+$14.5 & $+$0.39 & $+$0.20 & $+$0.15 & $-$0.77 & $-$2.87 & $+$0.39 \\
16 & 7.4\% & $+$17.7 & $+$0.78 & $+$0.24 & $-$0.02 & $-$0.06 & $-$3.95 & $+$0.78 \\
32 & 4.0\% & $+$20.0 & $+$0.95 & $+$0.35 & $+$0.65 & $+$0.22 & $-$4.35 & $+$0.95 \\
